// The stdlib LOADER — it enumerates every `.maxon` file under the checkout's `stdlib/`, top level and
// subdirectories alike, and registers each one into the query database exactly as a user source is —
// plus ONE temporary thing: a WHITELIST that filters which of those files are actually loaded while
// shv2 grows the language module by module.
//
// ⭐ **THE WHITELIST IS SCAFFOLDING AND IT IS MEANT TO DIE. THE LOADER IS NOT.** v1
// (`StdlibLoader.PrependStdlib`) and the bootstrap (`BuildCache.WithStdlibSources`) load ALL of
// `stdlib/`, and that is shv2's end state too — most of those files simply do not compile with shv2 yet
// (Map/FilePath/String-methods each have their own blockers, see PLAN.md). The filter bounds the blast
// radius until they do.
//
// It is written as a FILTER INSIDE the real loader, not as a list the loader walks, and that shape is
// the point: **the end state is a DELETION, not a rewrite.** Remove the two marked lines in
// `loadStdlibSources` and everything below the banner, and what is left already loads all of
// `stdlib/`. A loader that walked a hardcoded list would have to be written from scratch on that day.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING OUTSIDE THIS FILE MAY KNOW A WHITELIST EXISTS — and that includes owning a type, a
// constant or an error case on its behalf.** The deletion above must not leave a stranded arm in
// `Diagnostics.maxon` and a stranded printer in `Main.maxon`, so the missing-module case raises the
// ordinary `fileNotFound`; the directory walk is `Compiler.collectMaxonSources`, the user walk's own;
// and the path array is `MaxonSourcePathArray`, declared beside it. Everything downstream deals in two
// durable facts — "is this function STDLIB source or USER source?" and "is it reachable from `main`?" —
// computed in `StdlibSource.maxon`, and both stay true when the filter goes. A pass that reasoned in
// terms of "whitelisted" would have to be rewritten that day; a pass that reasons in terms of stdlib
// source does not notice.
//
// ⭐ HOW A LOADED MODULE REACHES USER CODE. Each file is registered into the query database
// (`fileChanged`) exactly like a user source, so it flows through the SAME tokenize → signature-index →
// parse → merge spine with no query-spine change. Its declarations therefore populate `funcSignatures`,
// and a user program can call `Clock.nowMs()` though the program itself contains no Clock.
//
// ⭐ **LOAD ORDER: WHATEVER THE FILESYSTEM SAYS. THE LOADER DOES NOT SORT, ON PURPOSE.** The bootstrap
// sorts its stdlib file list (`0-Compiler.cs:967` — `Interfaces.maxon` first, then helper
// subdirectories, then top level) because ITS front end needs declarations to have been seen before a
// use is compiled. **shv2 does not have that constraint**: `queryProgramSignatures` sweeps EVERY file's
// tokens for declarations before any file is parsed, so visibility is order-independent (ARCHITECTURE's
// query-spine section).
//
// ⚠ **AND SHV2 DOES NOT SORT FOR DETERMINISM EITHER — a sort here would be the WRONG FIX** (user
// ruling, 2026-07-24). Forcing a canonical input order does not remove an order dependence; it makes
// one *reliably hidden*, so a latent "this only works because the files arrived in this order" bug
// stays latent until the machine, the filesystem or a refactor changes the order. **The compiler must
// not depend on file order to work correctly**, and the way that stays true is by not constraining the
// input, so any dependence surfaces where it can be fixed. Where an ORDER genuinely matters it is
// canonicalized at the point of emission, from something stable — never by constraining what the
// loader was handed.
//
// ⭐ MEASURED, not assumed, by running the loaders in raw `Directory.list` order and again in
// DELIBERATELY REVERSED order (2026-07-24; the numbers are in this rung's optimization-log row):
//
//   • SEMANTICS DO NOT MOVE. Reversing the USER directory walk (`Compiler.loadSourceDirectory`, the one
//     that actually walks many files today) leaves all 1586 x64 tests and all 1394 wasm tests PASSING,
//     and reversing the stdlib walk over 48 modules — with the filter deleted, so the whole set loads —
//     produces a program that runs identically and Target IR that is a PURE PERMUTATION of the other
//     order's, the same lines in a different order. Nothing computes a different answer.
//
//   • ⚠ EMISSION ORDER DOES. Reversing the user walk moves 14 committed cross-file fragments: 13 are
//     `func @…` blocks in a different order, and one swaps which of two same-named FILE-PRIVATE globals
//     gets the `$1` disambiguating suffix — both assigned by the order the files were encountered. So
//     the emitted byte image is a function of the filesystem's order today. That is real and it is NOT
//     repaired by sorting the input, which would only hide it: the repair belongs at the point of
//     emission, deriving the order from something stable like the declaration name. Reported, not
//     papered over — see PLAN.md.
//
// ⚠ Two further things the bootstrap does that this does NOT, both deliberate:
//   • It EXCLUDES `stdlib/Internals.maxon` (the `__Internals.*` intrinsic namespace it does not
//     implement). shv2 does not implement it either, but the filter already excludes it and a second,
//     permanent exclusion for a case shv2 has not yet met would be an unmeasured guess. Whoever deletes
//     the filter meets `Internals.maxon` on that day and decides then.
//   • It CACHES the parsed stdlib module process-wide. shv2's caching is the query spine's
//     content-hash memo, which already covers these files because they are registered as ordinary
//     sources; a second cache would be the same fact stored twice.
//
// ⭐ THE ONE INVARIANT WHILE THE FILTER STANDS: adding a module changes NOTHING for a program that does
// not use it. A program that never calls Clock must compile to the exact bytes it did before Clock was
// listed — same x64 goldens, same wasm/arm64 output. Two mechanisms compose to guarantee that:
//
//   1. Dead-function elimination (`DeadFunctionElimination.eliminateDeadFunctions`) drops every stdlib
//      function no reachable code calls, at the target-neutral backend entry — so an unused Clock never
//      reaches isel/regalloc/encode and never lands in a golden, on any target.
//
//   2. ⚠ But DFE runs at the Std tier, AFTER `scanRuntimeUsage` has already decided which RUNTIME FLOOR
//      the program carries (heap / GT scheduler / wall clock) by walking the MERGED MAXON MODULE.
//      Clock.nowMs's body calls `__gt_now_ns` and WallClock.nowUnixSeconds's calls `__clock_now_unix_s`,
//      so a NAIVE load would make `scanRuntimeUsage` install the GT scheduler + the wall-clock `.data`
//      slots for a program that never reads a clock — slots DFE does not prune (it prunes FUNCTIONS,
//      never `.data`). MEASURED before that gate: a bare `return 7` grew from 1512 to 2488 code bytes
//      and gained 24 `.data` slots. `StdlibFacts.unreachable` is what closes it, and it is stated as
//      reachability rather than as membership precisely because the fix outlives this file.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE INVARIANT IS ABOUT CODE, AND IT CANNOT BE ABOUT DECLARED NAMES — MEASURED WHEN `File.maxon`
// WAS LISTED (R4.7 / N2).** Both mechanisms above prune what a program does not CALL. Neither can prune
// what a program does not SPELL, because a name is resolved before there is any code to eliminate: a
// `typealias` in a listed module is nameable from every program the moment the entry lands, and
// SO IS ITS RANGE. `stdlib/File.maxon:45` writes `export typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` and is the
// FIRST listed module to declare `Byte` at all (checked at the source against the nine entries before it
// — `helpers/url/urlHelpers.maxon`'s is `ByteValue`, a different name). A `b"…"` literal's element is
// that same user-visible `Byte` BY DESIGN (`SignatureIndex.isByteElementName`'s roster), so on that day a
// byte literal's element acquired declared BOUNDS for the first time and `a.set(0, value: n)` gained the
// narrowing check it had been missing — moving ONE committed golden,
// `byte-string-literal.detached-literal-drops-clean`, in a program that never mentions `File`. The move
// is a PURE INSERTION and the program's meaning is unchanged (its `n` is 0…4 against `0 to 255`, so the
// guard cannot fire; same stdout, same exit code) — but the emitted bytes DID move, and the sentence
// above says they may not.
//
// ⇒ **READ IT AS: adding a module changes nothing for a program that does not use it, EXCEPT through the
// names it DECLARES — and a declared name is not something a program opts into.** That is not a loophole
// to route around; it is the same door that supplies `ByteArray` to `byte-string-literal.top-level-let`,
// which this rung deliberately used. An entry may not be weighed on its FUNCTIONS alone: weigh every
// DECLARATION it carries, and expect a golden to move when one of them is a name the COMPILER ITSELF
// mints instances of.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND `export` IS NOT THE DISCRIMINATOR — SAYING IT WAS IS A RULE THE NEXT ENTRY WOULD FAIL BY
// (N2 review). MEASURED: delete the `export` from `stdlib/File.maxon:45` and the moved golden below is
// still emitted, byte-identically.** Two independent mechanisms make the keyword unreadable here, and
// either one alone is enough:
//   • `Parser.typeAliasVisibility` hands EVERY `typealias` in a stdlib source file `Visibility.global`,
//     `export`ed or not — so for a LISTED module the keyword is never consulted at all.
//   • The whole-program folds that actually carried the golden — `RangedAliasRegistry.widenStorageBytesFor`
//     ⇒ `storageBytesInEveryFile` (the stride) and `record`'s `multiplyDeclared` ⇒ `settleRangeContests`
//     (the range contest) — are keyed on the NAME and consult visibility NOWHERE. They fold every
//     declaration in the program, private ones included. This half holds for a USER file too.
// ⇒ **weigh every ranged `typealias` a candidate module declares, `export`ed or not.** An entry audited on
// its `export`s alone would wave a file-private `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` straight through, and it
// would move exactly the same goldens.
//
// ⭐ THE COLLISION RULE FOR ADDING AN ENTRY. A listed module must declare no name a user program
// declares and no builtin type/name.
//   • FREE-FUNCTION-name collisions — a user `function sleep` against `Sleep.maxon`'s, or two stdlib
//     modules — are caught LOUDLY by the whole-program duplicate-function check (E3006,
//     `commitFuncSignatures` in ParseStaging), because a stdlib file merges through the identical path a
//     user file does. Nothing to add here; `stdlib-whitelist.md` pins it.
//   • ⚠⚠ **TYPE-name collisions against a USER program ARE NO LONGER COLLISIONS — THE USER WINS AND THE
//     STDLIB DECLARATION MOVES (A1s wave 2).** A user `type`/`enum`/`union`/`interface`/ranged-`typealias`
//     spelled like a listed module's NOMINAL declaration shadows it: the stdlib one is renamed into the
//     reserved `__` space and the user keeps the bare name, so both live at once and each side's own code
//     sees its own. `specs-shv2/stdlib-user-shadows.md` is the rule, the measurement and the pins. ⇒ The
//     old warning here — "listing a module RESERVES its type names program-wide, so weigh an entry whose
//     names are ordinary English words" — IS RETIRED, and that is the point of the wave: `Clock`,
//     `ParseError` and `Promise` cost a user program nothing. A METHOD of a shadowed type moves with it
//     (`Clock.nowMs` becomes `__Clock.nowMs`), which is why the bullet above is about FREE functions.
//     ⚠ What DOES still refuse the program is a listed module using a contested spelling as a MEMBER
//     name — a field, a method or an argument label — because user code can reach a member and its tokens
//     are never rewritten. That is E3112, and it is checked, not assumed.
//   • TYPE-name collisions BETWEEN TWO LISTED MODULES are still caught LOUDLY by the merge, and shadowing
//     does not touch them: it is about PROVENANCE, and two stdlib declarations have the same one. Two
//     listed modules declaring one type name is a maintainer's bug in this list.
//   • TYPE-name-vs-BUILTIN collisions are the one shape still SILENT, and they remain the maintainer's
//     responsibility. shv2 has no builtin-type-redeclaration diagnostic — a user `type String` compiles
//     today as a distinct nominal, and the builtin names are not in the type-name registry at all — so
//     enforcing one is a general language matter, not a scaffolding one. Clock declares
//     `Clock`/`WallClock` and time typealiases, none of them builtin, so it cannot hit this. Do not list
//     a module that redeclares a builtin.
//   • ⚠ FUNCTION-name-vs-BARE-BUILTIN collisions are SILENT — which is why the builtin is RETIRED FIRST,
//     and `stdlib/Sleep.maxon` is the entry that was added that way. `Parser.parseCallNamed` recognizes a
//     set of BARE names — `print` and `trunc` among them — BEFORE `emitCall`
//     consults any registry, so while one of them claims a name, a CALL to that name never reaches a
//     declaration of it: listing such a module loads code no ordinary call site can reach — `sleep(5)`
//     still emitted the builtin's `__gt_sleep` — while charging the per-module load cost for zero
//     delivered capability, and the name has TWO routes, because `let f = sleep` is not a call site and
//     took the address of the declaration the call sites could not see.
//     ⚠ The shadowing was NOT this file's doing and predated it: a user file declaring its own
//     `function sleep` compiled with the declaration silently unlinked, no diagnostic and a wrong answer
//     (measured on plain user code). The repair was to delete the bare-name builtin and let the stdlib
//     declaration be authoritative, which moved 15 committed goldens across 7 specs — 14 gaining the
//     call frame `@sleep` now is, and one holding the builtin's bespoke argument rejection, replaced by
//     the ordinary one against `sleep(milliseconds Milliseconds)`. MEASURED, in a controlled A/B in one
//     worktree: the entry costs +683 allocations / +31,308 bytes on EVERY compile, identical at rung 0
//     and rung 5 of the scale ladder, and the emitted code of a program that never sleeps is
//     byte-identical (`codeBytes` equal at all six rungs — DFE prunes the module it does not reach).
//     ⇒ THE RULE: do not list a module whose function name a bare builtin claims. Retire the builtin
//     first.
//     ⚠ **AND CHECK THE ROSTER AT ITS SOURCE — `parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain — NEVER against a
//     list written here or in a spec.** This bullet is deliberately not one: nothing makes a prose roster
//     agree with the chain, and both prose copies of it were already wrong. They named four of the FIFTEEN
//     names standing after `sleep` left, omitting `spawnReadLine` and all seven `subp*` — so a maintainer
//     checking a module against the list rather than the chain would have been told a claimed name was
//     free, and got back exactly the silent shadow this rule exists to prevent.
//   • ⚠⚠ **A LISTED MODULE'S OWN BODY MUST NOT BE TYPE-PARAMETERIZED BY A USER DECLARATION — and this clause
//     is here because the four above did NOT cover it, twice.** Every bullet so far is about a NAME the
//     module DECLARES colliding with one the user declares. This one is about a TYPE the module's body
//     merely USES, resolving differently per program: `__ManagedMemory` is registered as an alias of
//     `Array with Byte`, and `Byte` is a name a user may declare — so a module whose body passes a
//     `__ManagedMemory` around stops compiling in any program that writes `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)`,
//     WITHOUT NAMING ANYTHING THE MODULE DOES. The module is parsed by every program, so the blast radius is
//     every program, and the diagnostic points at a stdlib line the author never opened.
//     ⚠ MEASURED TWICE, three rungs apart, with the same two `specs-shv2/bytearray-element-size.md` cases
//     and the same sentence — `E3005 argument type mismatch for 'managed': expected '__ManagedMemory', got
//     'Array_Byte'` — first inside `stdlib/URL.maxon` (BATCH2 slice 1), then inside `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`
//     at `String.init(managed)` (the command-line rung).
//     ⇒ THE RULE: a value a listed module receives from the COMPILER (an intrinsic's result, a synthesized
//     view) must carry a compiler-owned type, not one spelled out of user-declarable names.
//     `SignatureIndex.SynthesizedByteElementName` is that answer for bytes and carries the whole argument.
//     ⚠ **THE SHADOWING MECHANISM DOES NOT REACH THIS AND MUST NOT BE REACHED FOR.** A1s wave 2 rewrites the
//     identifier TOKENS of a listed module, which cures a name the module WRITES; `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`
//     never writes `Byte` at all. The fix belongs where the type is MINTED, not where it is spelled.
//     ⇒ CHECKING IT is a build, not a reading: compile a program that declares its own `Byte` as a wide
//     range and calls NOTHING in the module. If the module appears in the output, this clause has been hit.

// ⭐ **THE LOADER.** Enumerate `stdlib/` and register each module, in the order the walk found them
// (see the header on why it does not sort). Called by `loadProject`
// AFTER the user's own sources are loaded (so a directory build's output name still derives from a user
// file, not from a stdlib module, and an empty user project still fails with `noSourceFiles` rather than
// silently compiling only stdlib).
//
// ⚠ THE ENUMERATION IS `Compiler.collectMaxonSources` — the SAME function that walks the user's own
// root — because "which files under this directory are Maxon sources?" is one question and `stdlib/`
// is not entitled to its own answer to it. A second walk here would be free to drift on the extension,
// on the build manifest, and on every ignore rule either walk grows.
//
// ⚠ LOUD, NEVER SILENT. An unreadable module propagates `readError` naming it; a PARSE failure surfaces
// through the ordinary parse-diagnostic path (`queryParseOps` → `reportParseError`), which already
// renders the file path. There is no arm that drops a stdlib module quietly.
export function loadStdlibSources(project Project) throws CompileError
	// ⬇ WHITELIST (1 of 2) — the list, and the check that it names files this checkout actually has.
	// Delete this line with the rest of the filter.
	let listed = try whitelistedStdlibModules(project.stdlibDir)

	var found = MaxonSourcePathArray.create()
	_ = try collectMaxonSources(project.stdlibDir, into: found)
	let usersOwn = registeredPathsWhenTheRootIsInsideStdlib(project)

	for path in found 'each'
		// ⬇ WHITELIST (2 of 2) — the ONLY thing between this loader and all of `stdlib/`.
		// Delete this `if` and every stdlib module is loaded.
		if not listed.contains(path) 'notListed'
			continue
		end 'notListed'

		if usersOwn.contains(path) 'theUsersOwnFile'
			continue
		end 'theUsersOwnFile'

		try loadOneSourceFile(project, path: path)
		// RECORDED, because it is the answer to "which of this compile's files are stdlib source?" and
		// this loop is the one place that knows it for free — every path here came out of an enumeration
		// of `stdlib/`. Re-deriving it later by testing each source path against that directory costs
		// four String allocations a question (see StdlibSource's header).
		project.stdlibSourcePaths.push(path)
	end 'each'
end 'loadStdlibSources'

// ⚠ **A FILE THE PROJECT ALREADY REGISTERED MUST NOT BE LOADED A SECOND TIME**, and the one way a
// single file can be reached by BOTH walks is a user root that lies under `stdlib/` — which is exactly
// what `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Clock.maxon` is, the command that checks whether a module is ready to
// be listed.
//
// It matters because the query database keys a source on its `FilePath` and `FilePath.equals` compares
// the path TEXT (case-insensitively on Windows) rather than resolving it — so a relative
// `stdlib/Clock.maxon` and this loader's absolute spelling of the SAME FILE are two identities,
// `fileChanged` dedups neither against the other, and the file is parsed twice: every function in it
// then collides with ITSELF. MEASURED before this guard: FIVE `E3006 duplicate definition of function
// 'Clock.nowMs'`, each pointing at Clock's own lines. The ABSOLUTE spelling of the same command was
// already clean, which is what identifies the fault as the spelling and not the double load.
//
// ⚠ **ASKED ONCE PER COMPILE, OF THE ROOT** — every source the project loaded lies under it, so one
// `isInside` settles it for the whole compile and an ordinary project pays nothing per file and gets an
// empty set. That bound is the point and not an optimization: re-deriving stdlib-ness per FILE was
// measured at +60…+1,920 allocations across the scale ladder and rejected (StdlibSource's header), and
// making a compile's diagnostics carry resolved paths instead was measured HERE at +824…+5,009 — a
// guard that cost either would be that decision undone.
//
// The file stays the USER's: they named it as their project, so its functions are user source and a
// diagnostic raised in it belongs at its own lines — which are lines they asked to compile.
function registeredPathsWhenTheRootIsInsideStdlib(project Project) returns MaxonSourcePathSet
	var registered = MaxonSourcePathSet.create()

	// ⚠ `Project.rootIsUnderStdlibDirectory`, not a fourth spelling of the same `resolve` + `isInside`:
	// `Queries.livesUnderStdlibDirectory`'s soundness proof is a claim about this exact predicate holding
	// the same way here and in `isStdlibSourceFile`, so it is asked in ONE place. See its header.
	let cwd = Directory.currentPath()
	if not rootIsUnderStdlibDirectory(project, cwd: cwd) 'rootIsElsewhere'
		return registered
	end 'rootIsElsewhere'

	for path in project.db.sourcePaths 'each'
		registered.insert(path.resolve(cwd))
	end 'each'
	return registered
end 'registeredPathsWhenTheRootIsInsideStdlib'

// ============================================================================================
// ⬇ THE WHOLE OF THE SCAFFOLDING. Everything above this line is the real loader and stays.
// Delete from here down, plus the two marked lines in `loadStdlibSources`, and shv2 loads all of
// `stdlib/` — with nothing left to change in any other file.
// ============================================================================================

// The listed modules are a `MaxonSourcePathSet` — a SET rather than a list, because the membership
// test runs once per file under `stdlib/` and THIS LIST IS DESIGNED TO GROW, one entry per widening
// rung, until it names every file there. A scan would therefore cost O(files x listed), and each pair
// is about four String allocations (see the alias). MEASURED against the 49 files this checkout has:
// a 48-entry scan costs ~9,400 allocations a compile where the hash lookup costs ~98, flat in the
// list's length.
//
// The stdlib modules this rung loads, each named RELATIVE TO `stdlib/` and resolved against the very
// directory the loader enumerates — so both sides of the membership test are built by the same `join`
// off the same base, and a `/`-spelled entry matches an enumerated `\` path. Naming them relative to
// the CHECKOUT ROOT instead would spell the directory's own name a second time (`StdlibSource` already
// owns it as `StdlibDirName`), and a rename would leave this list silently pointing nowhere.
//
// ⭐ WIDENING IS ONE LINE: add another `listWhitelistedModule` call.
function whitelistedStdlibModules(stdlibDir FilePath) returns MaxonSourcePathSet throws CompileError
	var listed = MaxonSourcePathSet.create()
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Clock.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Sleep.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐ **P1.8 Slice E — the nine `utf16*` free functions**, which the corpus calls by BARE NAME
	// (`utf16IsLeadSurrogate(55357)`). shv2 has no bare-name free-function BUILTIN door at all — every
	// builtin is reached through a receiver-dot or a `Type.` qualifier — so a synthesized version of these
	// would have needed a whole new dispatch mechanism to be callable. Listing the module needs none: the
	// declarations flow through the ordinary signature index and the calls are ordinary calls.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule. Its nine function names are claimed by no bare
	// builtin (`Parser.parseCallNamed`'s chain) and by no other listed module; its two type names
	// (`CodeUnit16`, `Utf16UnitCount`) are RANGED typealiases, which are file-local per alias FORM and so
	// reserve nothing from a user program; and it redeclares no builtin type. It has no imports.
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS ONE BLOCKER WAS `Codepoint`, AND THE FIX IS NOT ANOTHER ENTRY HERE.** The parameter type is
	// declared in `stdlib/Character.maxon`, which shv2 cannot load: `Character` is a name the compiler owns,
	// and MEASURED, listing it is `E3005 Cannot return 'struct' from function declared to return
	// 'Character'` at that module's own `static function init`. So `Codepoint` is answered where the
	// compiler already answers for `HashValue` — `isSynthesizedIntAliasName`, whose header carries the
	// argument in full. With that, `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/helpers/string/utf16.maxon` compiles clean.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/string/utf16.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **A1s — THE RESERVED-SPACE MODULE, AND THE ONE ENTRY THAT BREAKS THREE OF THIS FILE'S OWN RULES
	// ON PURPOSE.** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` is where the language's compiler-facing declarations actually
	// live: the three `__Managed*Error` enums the runtime throws, `interface Parsable`, and the
	// `__int_fromString` / `__float_fromString` / `__bool_fromString` helpers. Listing it is what makes
	// those declarations exist in an ORDINARY program instead of being transcribed a second time inside
	// the compiler — this file's "one mention, one place" property applied to the stdlib itself.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE FIRST LISTED MODULE THAT CONTAINS LITERALS, AND THAT MADE A FILED DEBT DUE.**
	// `InsertRangeChecks.maxon:167-179` had already MEASURED that the byte-neutrality invariant above
	// "holds TODAY only because no listed module contains a literal, which is a property of the three
	// files listed and not of the mechanism". Measured here on the entry itself: this one line moved
	// **317 committed goldens** — `__fconst` (156), `__str_blob` (132), `__jumptable` (29) — for
	// declarations no user program reaches. Paying that debt is part of this rung, not a follow-on.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS THE FIRST LISTED MODULE A USER PROGRAM LEGITIMATELY REDECLARES OVER — WHICH WAVE 1
	// MEASURED AND WAVE 2 FIXED.** A user's own `enum ParseError` used to be `E3006` against this file's,
	// which is why `specs-shv2/parsable-interface.md`'s cases were disabled. It now SHADOWS: this file's
	// declaration moves to `__ParseError` and the user keeps the bare name. The paragraphs below are kept
	// verbatim because they are the MEASUREMENT that chose the mechanism, not a description of a gap.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE REASON IS WORTH MORE THAN THE SYMPTOM, because it kills the obvious fix.** The oracle
	// permits both at once — MEASURED: one program declaring `enum ParseError` AND calling
	// `int.fromString("41")` compiles and exits 42, so the user's declaration and the `ParseError` this
	// file's own `__int_fromString` throws are BOTH live. That is SCOPING, not a tie-break, and the
	// bootstrap gets it free from parse ORDER. **shv2 cannot: `queryProgramSignatures` folds every file
	// before any file is parsed, so there is no "already bound" state for a later file to shadow.**
	// Making the user's declaration simply WIN program-wide was built and measured, and it breaks THIS
	// FILE — `E3034: unknown enum case: 'invalidFormat'` raised inside `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`, whose
	// body names the case a dozen times. And shv2 identifies aggregates by NAME all the way down
	// (`structRef`, `aggregateNameFor`, `__destruct_<name>`), so two live declarations of one name are
	// not representable in the merged IR at all.
	//
	// ⇒ the cure was to RENAME the shadowed stdlib declaration behind the reserved prefix and redirect this
	// module's own references to it — the same trade `ProgramSignatures.reservedIfDeclared` makes one
	// namespace over. SHIPPED as A1s wave 2, applied to the stdlib file's identifier TOKENS at
	// `Parser.create` so every derived spelling follows by construction.
	// `specs-shv2/stdlib-user-shadows.md` carries the full measurement and the rule.
	//
	// ⚠ The module is named by `Queries.CompilerInternalDeclaringModule` and NOT by a literal here, because
	// that constant is what grants this same file the E2051 exemption its `__` declarations need — two
	// literals would be the listing and the exemption free to name different files. See its header.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: CompilerInternalDeclaringModule, into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **BATCH2 slice 1 — `URL`, AND THE FIRST ENTRY THAT IS A CONE RATHER THAN A FILE.**
	// `stdlib/URL.maxon` does not compile alone: its `parse`/`resolve` call SIX free functions declared in
	// `stdlib/helpers/url/urlHelpers.maxon` (`urlIsAlpha`, `urlIsDigit`, `urlIsSchemeChar`,
	// `urlValidatePercentEncoding`, `urlRemoveDotSegments`, `urlMergeBasePath`), and that file in turn names
	// `URLError`. The two are mutually dependent and are listed as ONE entry in two lines — order-independent
	// like every other, since `queryProgramSignatures` folds every file's declarations before any file parses.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each checked rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): the helper module's eight are all `url`-prefixed and are claimed by no other
	//     listed module and by no BARE builtin — checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s `if` chain AT THE
	//     CHAIN, which is what that rule's last bullet insists on. `URL.maxon` itself declares none: every one
	//     of its functions is a method or a static of `URL`.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `URL` and `URLError` are nominal declarations, so a user program spelling either wins and
	//     these move into the reserved space (A1s wave 2). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap
	//     (`Clock`/`WallClock`, `ParseError`/`Parsable`, `CodeUnit16`/`Utf16UnitCount`), and neither is a
	//     builtin type name, so the one SILENT shape cannot be hit.
	//   • `PortNumber` and `ByteValue` are RANGED typealiases, file-local per alias FORM, and reserve nothing.
	//   • ⚠ The E3112 MEMBER-name rule — the one shadowing does not rescue — is a whole-program CHECK
	//     (`Queries.refuseContestedNamesUsedAsMembers`) that fires only when a user program actually contests a
	//     spelling, and it now scans these two files like every other listed module. Nothing to clear ahead of
	//     time; what it protects is real, because `URL` carries ordinary English members (`path`, `host`,
	//     `port`, `query`, `fragment`) and a user type spelled like one of them is refused rather than silently
	//     half-renamed.
	//
	// ⚠ Its blockers were TWO `String` methods, and neither was a missing type: `addressableBytes()` and
	// `byteAtOrPanic()` are `module function`s of `stdlib/String.maxon` — a file shv2 does not and will not
	// parse, since `String` is compiler-owned — so they are answered as dispatch arms gated on the caller's
	// file being physically under `stdlib/` (`Parser.requireStdlibOnlyStringMethod`), which is what keeps a
	// user program's `"x".addressableBytes()` refused exactly as the oracle refuses it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT GATE MUST READ `livesUnderStdlibDirectory`, NEVER `isStdlibSource`** — the first version read
	// the latter and broke `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/URL.maxon`, i.e. the command THIS FILE's collision rule
	// prescribes for checking a module before listing it. See `Queries.livesUnderStdlibDirectory`.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "URL.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "helpers/url/urlHelpers.maxon", into: listed)
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "FilePath.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐ `stdlib/Math.maxon` — `Math.sin/cos/exp/log/log10/log2/tan/pow/atan/atan2`, every one of them PURE
	// MAXON (Taylor series over `float`). No libm, no intrinsic and no backend work: the module is loadable
	// exactly as written, which is why the entry is one line and not a rung.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE. All ten of its functions are `static`s of `Math`, so the
	//     whole free-function clause — and with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost `Sleep.maxon` a
	//     builtin retirement — is vacuous here. Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain
	//     AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last bullet insists: nothing this module declares can be claimed by it,
	//     because it declares nothing the chain can see.
	//   • ⚠ It is instead the first listed module that CONSUMES one: `Math.pow` calls `floor(exponent)` by bare
	//     name, which the chain answers with `MaxonUnaryOp.floor`. That direction is sound and is what the
	//     builtins are for — what a bare builtin reserves is a DECLARATION no call site can reach, and there is
	//     no declaration here.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Math` is a nominal declaration, so a user program spelling it wins and this one moves
	//     into the reserved space (A1s wave 2). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap, checked
	//     the way the bullet above insists a roster be checked — AT THE SOURCE, by reading the declarations of
	//     the modules the calls above name, not against a roster transcribed here: no listed module declares
	//     `Math` or `Real`. (A prose list of the other entries' type names is the same thing written down
	//     twice, and the two copies in this file had already drifted apart.)
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: `Math` is not a builtin. It appears NOWHERE in
	//     shv2's own sources — the five math intrinsics are BARE names (`floor`/`ceil`/`round`/`sqrt`/`abs`),
	//     never `Math.`-qualified — which is also why `Math.exp` reaches `emitCall` as an ordinary qualified
	//     callee instead of being intercepted by `parseCallNamed`'s `__Builtins.`-prefixed intrinsic table.
	//   • `Real` is a RANGED typealias, file-local per alias FORM, so it reserves nothing from a user program.
	//     Its form `float(f64.min to f64.max)` is already declared under a second name by a listed module
	//     (`Builtins.maxon`'s `ParsedFloat`) — the same same-form-different-name shape `Clock`'s four
	//     `int(0 to u64.max)` aliases and `Sleep.maxon`'s `Milliseconds` have carried since those entries.
	//   • The E3112 MEMBER-name rule is a whole-program CHECK that fires only when a user program actually
	//     contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear ahead of time. `Math` has no fields at all — it is
	//     a statics-only type — so the surface it exposes to that rule is just the ten method names.
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other module.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes:
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Math.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE —
	// the module parses and analyzes clean under shv2 and fails only for not being a program.
	//
	// ⭐ It is the second listed module to contain LITERALS (after `Builtins.maxon`, whose **317 goldens** are
	// recorded above), and it carries 14 distinct float constants. **MEASURED on this entry: ZERO committed
	// goldens moved** — 3422/0 with no fragment but this spec's own newly minted one. That is not luck and it
	// is the first independent confirmation that the debt `Builtins.maxon` came due on was actually PAID:
	// `InsertRangeChecks` and `lowerMaxonToStd` both skip an UNREACHABLE stdlib body (`StdlibFacts.unreachable`,
	// with `eliminateDeadFunctions.requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` proving the skip), so a literal in a
	// function no path from `main` reaches now registers no `.rdata` and no `__fconst` slot to displace anyone
	// else's. A module listed before that repair would have moved goldens here; this one cannot.
	//
	// ✅ **THE WRONG ANSWER THIS ENTRY ONCE SHIPPED IS FIXED (2026-08-02), and the note is kept because the
	// shape it describes is the reason the listing was safe all along.** This entry used to read "LOADABLE
	// IS NOT CORRECT": `Math.exp` holds `is_negative = val < 0.0` across the `while val > 1.0` reduction
	// loop and re-tests it at `invert`, and shv2 answered `Math.exp(-2.0) >= 1.0` — the reciprocal silently
	// skipped — where the bootstrap answered `< 1.0`.
	//
	// The fault was never in this module or in the whitelist. It was **compare/branch fusion proved PER
	// BLOCK but recorded PER VALUE** in `StdToX64Conversion` — a lossy key, so the second `if is_negative`
	// emitted a bare `jcc` on flags left by the squaring loop's INTEGER counter compare, with no `cmp` and
	// no `setcc` anywhere. Fixed by keying the map on `BlockRef`; a block has exactly one terminator, so the
	// key cannot be lossy.
	//
	// ⚠ **`exp` is the ONLY function here with that shape** — checked, not assumed: `is_negative` occurs at
	// three lines in this stdlib module, all inside `exp`. `atan` folds its negative case by immediate
	// recursion, `log`/`log2` hold no boolean local, and `sin`/`cos` never compare against `0.0`. They were
	// never wrong because they never had the shape, which is a different claim from being covered by a fix.
	//
	// MEASURED after the fix, both compilers agreeing: `Math.exp(-2.0)` is `0.13533528323661265` under shv2
	// and `0.135335` under the bootstrap (same double, different formatter). ⇒ **the primary pin is
	// `specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md`'s `one-compare-two-branches-across-a-loop`**, built by the fix's
	// own commit — integer-only, no float, no stdlib, so it holds whatever this module does next.
	// `specs-shv2/exp.md`'s `negative` case (`trunc(Math.exp(-1.0))` is `0`; the old lowering gave `2`, the
	// un-inverted `e`) is a SECONDARY end-to-end confirmation that arrived later by the whitelist — real,
	// but it would stop pinning silently if `exp`'s algorithm were rewritten.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Math.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/CommandLine.maxon` — `CommandLine.args()`, `optionValue`, `getOptionValue`.** The first
	// listed module whose leaves are OS intrinsics rather than pure Maxon: `args()` is a loop over
	// `__Builtins.commandLineCount()` / `__Builtins.commandLineArg(i)`, which lower to the `__cl_*` runtime
	// (`Runtime/CommandLineRuntime.maxon` — one `GetCommandLineA` import and the CRT split).
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE. All three of its functions are `static`s of `CommandLine`,
	//     so the whole free-function clause — and with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost
	//     `Sleep.maxon` a builtin retirement — is vacuous here. Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s
	//     bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last bullet insists: nothing this module declares
	//     can be claimed by it, because it declares nothing the chain can see.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `CommandLine` is a nominal declaration, so a user program spelling it wins and this one
	//     moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap,
	//     checked AT THE SOURCE by reading the declarations of the modules the calls above name — no listed
	//     module declares `CommandLine`.
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: `CommandLine` is not a builtin. It appears NOWHERE
	//     in shv2's own sources as a type — the two intrinsics it bottoms out in are `__Builtins.`-qualified,
	//     which is a DIFFERENT namespace (`BuiltinsQualifierPrefix`), and the runtime entries are `__cl_`.
	//     ⚠ Checked the harder way too, against `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`, since this rung ADDS
	//     a name to that list (`StringError`): `CommandLine` is not on it and cannot be, because nothing in
	//     the compiler synthesizes a declaration of it.
	//   • `StringArray` is a `typealias … = Array with String` — a GENERIC-INSTANCE alias, file-local per alias
	//     FORM like `Clock`'s ranged ones, so it reserves nothing from a user program.
	//   • The E3112 MEMBER-name rule is a whole-program CHECK that fires only when a user program actually
	//     contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear ahead of time. The surface it exposes is the three
	//     method names (`args`, `optionValue`, `getOptionValue`) — `CommandLine` is a statics-only type with no
	//     fields at all.
	//   • It has NO imports and calls into no other stdlib module.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THREE BLOCKERS HAD TO GO FIRST, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS THE INTRINSICS** — the whitelist is
	// ALL-OR-NOTHING PER FILE, which is a property worth stating because it is not obvious and it was
	// MEASURED: with this line present and the intrinsics absent, `function main() returns int / return 7`
	// was refused with `E3004 … stdlib\CommandLine.maxon:9:15`, i.e. a program that never mentions
	// `CommandLine` died on a function it never calls. `StdlibFacts.unreachable` prunes LOWERING, not PARSING.
	// So `optionValue`'s two obstacles were this entry's obstacles as much as `args()`'s were:
	//   1. `String.indexAfter` did not exist — now built (`__strix_after`, a reuse of `__strix_advance`);
	//   2. `throw StringError.notFound` did not parse, because `StringError` is declared in
	//      `stdlib/String.maxon`, which this loader can never list (`String` is compiler-owned). It is now
	//      SYNTHESIZED, exactly as `ArrayError` already was for the identical provenance
	//      (`Project.builtinStringErrorEnum`).
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes:
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/CommandLine.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING
	// ELSE — the module parses and analyzes clean under shv2 and fails only for not being a program.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE FIRST LISTED MODULE THAT IS x64-WINDOWS-ONLY, and the gate for that is NOT here.**
	// `SemanticCheck.stdlibSubstrateEntries` propagates `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` through the stdlib call
	// graph, so a wasm/arm64 program calling `CommandLine.args()` is refused (E3104) at ITS OWN call site,
	// while a program that never calls it is unaffected on every target — the reachability asymmetry that
	// function's header records. Listing the module is what makes those calls exist; it is not what gates them.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "CommandLine.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/File.maxon` — THE LAST LINK OF THE `URL → FilePath → File` CONE (R4.7)**, and the entry
	// that finally gives a program `File.readText`/`writeText`/`readBinary`/`writeBinary`/`exists`/`delete`/
	// `rename`/`info`. Every public signature takes a `FilePath`, which is why the two entries above had to
	// land first; it imports nothing else and calls only `__ManagedFile` builtins and `String`.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE. All fourteen of its functions are methods or statics of
	//     `FileInfo`, `FileReader`, `FileWriter` or `File`, so the whole free-function clause — and with it the
	//     SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost `Sleep.maxon` a builtin retirement — is vacuous here. Checked
	//     against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last bullet
	//     insists: nothing this module declares can be claimed by it, because it declares nothing the chain can
	//     see. Nor does it CONSUME one, the way `Math.pow` consumes `floor`.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `File`, `FileInfo`, `FileReader`, `FileWriter`, `FilePermission` and the five error enums
	//     (`FileReadError`, `FileWriteError`, `FileDeleteError`, `FileRenameError`, `FileInfoError`) are nominal
	//     declarations, so a user program spelling any of them wins and this module's moves into the reserved
	//     space (A1s wave 2, now directory-scoped by N1). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap,
	//     checked the way the bullet above insists a roster be checked — AT THE SOURCE, by reading those
	//     modules' own declarations rather than a roster transcribed here. ⚠ `FilePath.maxon` is the near miss
	//     and it is a miss: it declares `FilePath` and `FilePathError`, neither of which this module declares.
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: none of the ten is a builtin. The `__Managed*`
	//     names this module CALLS are builtins and are `__`-reserved, and it declares no name beginning `__`.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **`FileSize`, `Timestamp` and `Byte` ARE RANGED typealiases (`:23`, `:24`, `:45`) — SO THE
	//     "file-local per alias FORM" ARGUMENT EVERY ENTRY SINCE `Clock` HAS LEANED ON DOES NOT APPLY TO
	//     THEM, AND SAYING IT DID IS WHAT MADE A MOVED GOLDEN LOOK LIKE A SURPRISE.** ⚠ It is that they are
	//     DECLARED and not that they are `export`ed: all three happen to carry the keyword, but it is not
	//     read here (MEASURED, N2 review — see the loader header's second ⛔ paragraph, which names the two
	//     mechanisms that ignore it). A ranged alias a listed module declares is nameable from every program
	//     and its bounds join the whole-program folds either way; MEASURED, a user program that
	//     writes `function widen(b Byte) returns Byte` and mentions nothing else of this module compiles under
	//     BOTH compilers and answers identically, which is the agreement listing it buys. `FileSize` and
	//     `Timestamp` cost nothing beyond the name — no compiler-minted instance has their element — but
	//     `Byte` is the element the compiler mints `Array with Byte` over, so its BOUNDS reach a `b"…"`
	//     literal in a program that never opens this file. That is the golden below, and the loader header's
	//     ⛔ paragraph is the general statement of it.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **`ByteArray` IS THE ONE NAME WORTH STOPPING ON, AND IT COINCIDES WITH THE COMPILER RATHER THAN
	//     COLLIDING WITH IT.** `export typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte` names the EXACT instance
	//     `__ManagedMemory` is already bound to — `parseTypeReference` folds the reserved spelling into the very
	//     `genericInstance(Array with Byte)` this alias resolves to (`ManagedMemoryTypeName`'s header, and
	//     `ReturnShapeSurface`, which exists precisely because the two spellings are ONE type). So this is not a
	//     second declaration of a compiler-owned name: it is a user-writable second SURFACE on one record, told
	//     apart by provenance (`Parser.valueIsBufferSurface`), and any program could already write it. Listing
	//     the module supplies the name three disabled spec cases were waiting on.
	//   • The E3112 MEMBER-name rule — the one shadowing does not rescue — is a whole-program CHECK that fires
	//     only when a user program actually contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear ahead of time.
	//     What it protects is real here: `FileInfo` carries ordinary English members (`size`, `modifiedTime`,
	//     `isDirectory`), and a user type spelled like one of them is refused rather than silently half-renamed.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes:
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/File.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING ELSE.
	//
	// ⚠ **EXACTLY ONE COMMITTED GOLDEN MOVES, AND IT IS A `b"…"` CASE THAT NEVER MENTIONS `File` — AN EXPORTED
	// NAME, NOT A LITERAL THIS MODULE EMITS.** `byte-string-literal.detached-literal-drops-clean` gains three
	// instructions: with `Byte` UNDECLARED a `b"…"` literal's element has no declared range, so
	// `a.set(0, value: n)` got NO narrowing check; `export typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)` (`:45`) finally
	// gives it one, and the slot that held `movRegReg rcx, r12` now holds `cmpRegImm32 rbx, 0` /
	// `cmpRegImm32 rbx, 255` / `mrt_panic`. A missing check now EMITTED, and the run is unchanged — that case's
	// own prose carries the A/B.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROUTE IS THE EXPORT AND NOT THE LITERALS, WHICH MATTERS BECAUSE THE LITERAL BOOKKEEPING WOULD
	// HAVE ACQUITTED THIS ENTRY.** `Builtins.maxon`, `Math.maxon` and `CommandLine.maxon` all carry literals
	// and `Math.maxon` measured ZERO moved goldens, which is the `StdlibFacts.unreachable` repair working
	// exactly as recorded. It works here too — no `.rdata` of this module's displaces anyone — and the golden
	// moved anyway, through a door that mechanism does not stand in. See the ⛔ paragraph in this file's
	// header.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "File.maxon", into: listed)
	// ⭐⭐ **`stdlib/Directory.maxon` — THE LAST LINK OF THE `URL → FilePath → File → Directory` CONE
	// (R4.8)**, and the entry that gives a program `Directory.list`/`exists`/`isDirectory`/`create`/
	// `currentPath`. Every public signature takes or returns a `FilePath`, which is why that entry had to
	// land first; it imports nothing else and calls only `__ManagedDirectory` builtins, `FilePath` and
	// `String`.
	//
	// It clears every entry in this file's collision rule, each CHECKED rather than assumed:
	//   • FREE FUNCTIONS (E3006): it declares NONE — all five functions are `static`s of `Directory` — so
	//     the whole free-function clause, and with it the SILENT bare-builtin shadow that cost
	//     `Sleep.maxon` a builtin retirement, is vacuous here. Checked against `Parser.parseCallNamed`'s
	//     bare-name `if` chain AT THE CHAIN, as that rule's last bullet insists, and in BOTH directions:
	//     this module declares nothing the chain can see, and it CONSUMES nothing the chain claims either
	//     — every call in its body is receiver-dot or `Type.`-qualified, the way `Math.pow` consumes
	//     `floor` and this module does not.
	//   • TYPE NAMES: `Directory` and `DirectoryListError` are nominal declarations, so a user program
	//     spelling either wins and this module's moves into the reserved space (A1s wave 2, directory-scoped
	//     by N1). Against the other listed modules there is no overlap, checked AT THE SOURCE by reading
	//     those modules' own declarations rather than a roster transcribed here. ⚠ `File.maxon` is the near
	//     miss and it is a miss twice over: it declares neither name, and its `FileInfo.isDirectory` is a
	//     MEMBER spelling, which the bullet below is about and this one is not.
	//   • TYPE-NAME-vs-BUILTIN, the one shape still SILENT: neither name is a builtin. `__ManagedDirectory`
	//     — the builtin this module wraps — is a DIFFERENT name and is `__`-reserved, and this module
	//     declares no name beginning `__`. Checked the harder way too, at both registries that can answer
	//     for a bare type name: `Directory` is on neither `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` nor
	//     `builtinTypeNameTag`, and nothing in the compiler synthesizes a declaration of it.
	//   • ⚠ **THE RANGED-TYPEALIAS CLAUSE THAT MOVED A GOLDEN FOR `File.maxon` IS VACUOUS HERE, AND THAT IS
	//     A DECLARATION COUNT RATHER THAN A HOPE.** This module declares exactly ONE typealias —
	//     `FilePathArray = Array with FilePath` (`:12`) — and it is a GENERIC-INSTANCE alias, file-local per
	//     alias FORM exactly as `CommandLine`'s `StringArray` is, so it reserves nothing from a user program
	//     and joins neither of the whole-program folds (`storageBytesInEveryFile`, `settleRangeContests`)
	//     that carried `File.maxon`'s golden. There is no ranged alias here for a `b"…"` literal in an
	//     unrelated program to reach. ⚠ Weighed on the DECLARATION and not on the `export` keyword, which
	//     this file's header measured to be unreadable at both mechanisms: the alias is file-private and
	//     would have counted just the same had it been ranged.
	//   • ⚠⚠ **THE "TYPE-PARAMETERIZED BY A USER DECLARATION" CLAUSE IS THE ONE THAT HAD TO BE BUILT RATHER
	//     THAN READ, AND THIS MODULE HAS THE EXACT SHAPE THAT BROKE THE LAST TWO.** Its body passes
	//     `__ManagedMemory` values around twice — `pattern.addressableBytes()` (`:19`) and
	//     `String.init(search.dir.filename())` (`:25`) — which is where `stdlib/URL.maxon` and then
	//     `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon` each died with `E3005 … expected '__ManagedMemory', got 'Array_Byte'`
	//     in any program declaring its own wide `Byte`. That clause says checking it is a BUILD and not a
	//     reading, so it was built: a program declaring `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)` that calls
	//     NOTHING in this module compiles and runs clean, because `SignatureIndex.SynthesizedByteElementName`
	//     MINTS that element instead of spelling it out of user-declarable names.
	//   • The E3112 MEMBER-name rule — the one shadowing does not rescue — is a whole-program CHECK that
	//     fires only when a user program actually contests a spelling, so there is nothing to clear ahead of
	//     time. The surface it exposes is five method names, one field (`dir`) and one argument label
	//     (`path`). Counted against what the listed set ALREADY contests rather than against this module's
	//     own roster: `exists`, `create`, `isDirectory` and `path` are all `File.maxon`'s already
	//     (`File.exists`, `FileInfo.create`, `FileInfo.isDirectory`, and `path` as the label of six of its
	//     statics), so the spellings genuinely NEW to the listed set are just `list`, `currentPath` and
	//     `dir`.
	//
	// MEASURED before listing, which is the check this file's rule prescribes:
	// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Directory.maxon` exits 1 with `E3001: No 'main' function found` AND NOTHING
	// ELSE — the module parses and analyzes clean under shv2 and fails only for not being a program.
	//
	// ⚠ **ZERO COMMITTED GOLDENS MOVE ON THIS ENTRY, and that is an A/B rather than a clean `git status`**
	// — which cannot answer the question at all (see this file's header and `.claude/CLAUDE.md`). MEASURED
	// in ONE worktree by building the same tree with and without this single line and running
	// `--filter=array-init/` against each: **16 fragments drift either way, and the runner's whole drift
	// block is BYTE-IDENTICAL between the two runs**, down to the register in every
	// `cmpRegImm32 r13, 0` → `movRegReg r8, r13`. Those 16 are inherited from the merge base and belong to
	// something else; this entry's contribution to them is nil. `array-init` is the right probe precisely
	// because it is the family that would notice: it is built on `Array with Byte` and on adoption, so it
	// reads both doors an entry can move a golden through — the `.rdata` one `StdlibFacts.unreachable`
	// closes, and the DECLARED-NAME one it does not, which is what `File.maxon`'s ranged `Byte` went
	// through.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS x64-WINDOWS-ONLY, AND THE GATE FOR THAT IS NOT HERE.** `isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee` is
	// already inside `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, so a wasm/linux/arm64 program that reaches
	// `Directory.list` is refused (E3104) at ITS OWN call site, while a program that never calls one is
	// unaffected on every target. Listing the module is what makes those calls exist; it is not what gates
	// them — which is why `specs-shv2/directory.md` carries the same `<!-- targets: x64-windows -->` marker
	// `file-io.md` documents, and points at that file for the reason.
	try listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir, relative: "Directory.maxon", into: listed)
	return listed
end 'whitelistedStdlibModules'

// One listed module. A listed module the checkout does not have is a hard error naming it, never a
// silent skip: the whole point of the list is that what it names is present.
//
// It raises the SAME `fileNotFound` a user's own missing input raises, and deliberately not a
// scaffolding-specific arm of its own. A private error case would be a second thing that has to be
// deleted the day the filter goes — in `Diagnostics.maxon` and again in `Main.maxon`'s printer —
// which is exactly the "one mention, one place" property this file exists to keep. The fact is the
// same either way: a path this compile was told to load is not there.
function listWhitelistedModule(stdlibDir FilePath, relative String, into MaxonSourcePathSet) throws CompileError
	let path = stdlibDir.join(relative)
	if not File.exists(path) 'missing'
		throw CompileError.fileNotFound(path)
	end 'missing'

	into.insert(path)
end 'listWhitelistedModule'
